# Production build config — copy to `.env.production` for a local release build.
#
# `.env.production` itself is gitignored. It used to be committed, and the cost
# was not secrecy — every VITE_* value is compiled into the renderer bundle and
# ships inside the installer, so none of it can be secret by construction — but
# a SECOND source of truth. CI already supplies these from repository variables,
# and the release workflow's guard reads only those, so a committed file could
# make the build log say "installers point at localhost" while the file had in
# fact set a real origin.
#
# ── Where the real values live ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   GitHub > Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions > Variables
#
#   VITE_BACKEND_ORIGIN   required — release.yml FAILS without it, before the
#                         build runs, rather than shipping installers wired to
#                         http://localhost:4000
#   VITE_MEDIA_ORIGINS    optional
#
# Nothing secret belongs in this file or in `.env.production`. Anything the
# server must keep — API keys, signing material, database URLs — belongs in the
# backend's own environment or in Actions *secrets*, never in a VITE_ variable.

# Where the packaged app talks to motion-back. Without it the app falls back to
# http://localhost:4000, which is right for development and useless in a build
# you hand to someone else.
VITE_BACKEND_ORIGIN=https://motion-back-production.up.railway.app

# Extra origins the renderer may load media from, comma-separated. Optional.
# VITE_MEDIA_ORIGINS=

# Object Matte — neural one-click subject selection in the Roto tool. Optional.
# A hosted SAM-class ONNX mask decoder (the prompt-encoder/decoder half that
# takes image embeddings + click coordinates; see samOnnxLoader.ts for the
# input names it expects). The runtime (onnxruntime-web) is installed; this
# is the only missing piece. Absent, the Roto tool stays on classical GrabCut.
# VITE_SAM_MODEL_URL=https://your-cdn.example/sam_vit_b_decoder.onnx
